<header><p class="st-kicker">kingdom · West Africa</p><h1 class="st-title">Kantora</h1><p class="st-meta">1500 CE – 1880 CE (approx.)</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Mandinka kingdom of the upper Gambia river, whose riverside market was the furthest point reached by Portuguese ships in the 15th century.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Kantora was one of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mandinka">Mandinka</a> states established along the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/the-gambia">Gambia</a> as the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/mali-empire">Mali Empire</a> extended its influence west. It occupied the south bank of the upper river around present-day Fatoto and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/basse-santa-su">Basse</a>. The Portuguese explorer <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/diogo-gomes">Diogo Gomes</a> reached its market, which he called Cantor, around 1456.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Kantora's rulers controlled the highest navigable point on the river, where caravans from the upper <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/niger-river">Niger</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bambuk">Bambuk</a> brought gold, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ivory-trade">ivory</a> and slaves to trade for salt, cloth and European goods. It later fell within the orbit of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/kaabu-empire">Kaabu Empire</a>, and Muslim traders settled in its towns.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>In the 1860s and 1870s Kantora was conquered by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fula">Fula</a> leader <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alfa-molo-balde">Alfa Molo</a> and his son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/musa-molo">Musa Molo</a>, becoming part of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/fuladu">Fuladu</a>. The area was divided between <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/gambia-colony-and-protectorate">British Gambia</a> and <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/french-senegal">French Senegal</a> by the boundary of 1889.</p><p class="st-conf">Few sources survive for this state; details are uncertain.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><div class="st-emblem" role="img" aria-label="kingdom, 1500 CE – 1880 CE (approx.)"><svg viewBox="0 0 100